%I A109455 #13 Oct 10 2026 04:06:49
%S A109455 2,4,10,34,178,1720,38456,2490634,550112272,448764716674
%N A109455 Number of equivalence classes of threshold functions under permutations of the variables.
%C A109455 a(n) = (1/n!) * Sum_{sigma in S_n} F(sigma), where F(sigma) is the number of threshold functions fixed by sigma. A threshold function fixed by sigma has a weight vector constant on the cycles of sigma (average any realizing weight vector over the cyclic group generated by sigma), so F(sigma) equals the number of threshold functions on the integer grid {0..l_1} X ... X {0..l_k}, where l_1, ..., l_k are the cycle lengths of sigma. For sigma = identity this is A000609(n). - _James Guillochon_, Sep 26 2026
%C A109455 A000609(n)/n! <= a(n) <= 2^n*A000617(n), since each NP-equivalence class splits into at most 2^n classes under permutations alone. - _James Guillochon_, Sep 26 2026
%D A109455 D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, Vol. 4A, Section 7.1.1, p. 79. [Gives incorrect a(6)=590440.]
%D A109455 S. Muroga, Threshold Logic and Its Applications. Wiley, NY, 1971. [Background]
%H A109455 James Guillochon, <a href="https://github.com/guillochon/oeis-results/tree/main/A109455">Python and Rust programs</a> (Burnside over cycle types, with an exact count of threshold functions on grids).
%Y A109455 Cf. A000609, A000617, A001529, A002078.
%K A109455 nonn,hard,more
%O A109455 0,1
%A A109455 _Don Knuth_, Aug 17 2005
%E A109455 a(6) corrected and a(7)-a(9) from _James Guillochon_, Sep 26 2026
